q('Severe CAP follows a hotel stay. Legionella is suspected, but the urinary antigen test is negative. Which diagnostic step is most appropriate?', 'Obtain lower respiratory Legionella PCR or culture while continuing indicated treatment',[
 ('Exclude every Legionella species on the negative urine result','The usual urine assay primarily detects L. pneumophila serogroup 1.'),('Use a single acute antibody titer as definitive exclusion','Serology is not a reliable early exclusion test.'),('Delay treatment until an environmental water result is available','Environmental investigation must not delay clinical therapy.')],
 'Combine urine and lower respiratory testing', '**A negative urinary antigen does not exclude all legionellosis.**',['Lower respiratory testing detects a broader range of types.','Culture also enables epidemiological comparison with an environmental source.'], '**Treat severe disease promptly while obtaining specimens.**',['A macrolide or appropriate respiratory fluoroquinolone supplies Legionella activity.','Do not delay treatment for sample collection.'],[['Urine antigen','Usually L. pneumophila serogroup 1'],['Respiratory PCR/culture','Broader diagnostic reach'],['Timing','Prompt treatment and sampling']],[LE],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient has Legionella pneumonia confirmed by appropriate testing. Which single-agent option has appropriate directed activity?', 'Levofloxacin',[
 ('Cefazolin','This is not a reliable directed Legionella treatment.'),('Vancomycin','It does not provide effective Legionella therapy.'),('Metronidazole','Its anaerobic activity does not address this intracellular respiratory pathogen.')],
 'Directed treatment must reach the relevant pathogen', '**Respiratory fluoroquinolones and macrolides are established active options.**',['Choose according to severity, contraindications and local guidance.','Beta-lactam therapy alone may fail to supply appropriate atypical activity.'], '**Review response and complications.**',['Severe disease or immunocompromise may require a different duration.','A positive diagnostic result can also trigger public health investigation.'],[['Active options','Respiratory fluoroquinolone or macrolide'],['Beta-lactam alone','Not reliable directed therapy'],['Follow-up','Clinical response and epidemiology']],[LE,CA])
q('A ventilated patient develops new fever, purulent secretions, worsening oxygenation and a new infiltrate 5 days after intubation. Which syndrome best fits?', 'Ventilator-associated pneumonia',[
 ('Community-acquired pneumonia by timing alone','This new event began after prolonged intubation.'),('Ventilator-associated tracheobronchitis as the best label','The new infiltrate and oxygenation deterioration support parenchymal infection.'),('Asymptomatic airway colonization alone','The clinical deterioration makes colonization alone an inadequate explanation.')],
 'VAP requires a new pneumonia syndrome after intubation', '**The conventional timing is more than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation.**',['No single bedside finding is fully specific.','Exclude edema, atelectasis and other causes of a new opacity.'], '**Collect respiratory microbiology and use the local antibiogram.**',['Prior antibiotics and resistance risks affect empiric coverage.','Narrow therapy when reliable results permit.'],[['Timing','More than 48 hours after intubation'],['Evidence','New infiltrate plus infectious syndrome'],['Treatment','Local risk-based empiric therapy then reassessment']],[HV],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with uncomplicated VAP improves promptly on effective therapy and culture results allow narrowing. What is the usual guideline-based treatment approach?', 'De-escalate to active targeted treatment and use about 7 days, adjusted to the clinical course',[
 ('Maintain all empiric drugs for 21 days despite susceptibility results','Routine prolonged broad treatment increases avoidable harm.'),('Stop after one day because fever has resolved','That does not constitute adequate treatment of established VAP.'),('Keep dual gram-negative therapy solely because it was used empirically','Definitive treatment can often be narrowed when susceptibilities and clinical status permit.')],
 'Empiric breadth is not a commitment to prolonged broad treatment', '**Seven days is the usual HAP/VAP course when recovery is appropriate.**',['The rate of improvement and complications can change duration.','A persistent radiographic opacity alone does not establish active infection.'], '**Use microbiology to reduce unnecessary exposure.**',['Confirm that the selected agent is active and appropriately dosed.','Ongoing shock, poor specimens or uncontrolled infection may alter the plan.'],[['Empiric phase','Risk-based coverage'],['Definitive phase','Targeted active therapy'],['Typical duration','7 days with clinical adjustment']],[HV],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A person with advanced HIV develops subacute dry cough, exertional dyspnea, hypoxemia and diffuse ground-glass opacities. Which diagnosis is most characteristic?', 'Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia',[
 ('Simple bacterial lobar pneumonia','It more often causes a focal acute consolidation, though overlap exists.'),('Isolated upper airway candidiasis','It does not explain diffuse hypoxemic lung disease.'),('Pulmonary embolism proven by the CT pattern','Ground-glass opacity is nonspecific and does not prove embolism.')],
 'Recognize the opportunistic diffuse pneumonia phenotype', '**Pneumocystis is a key diagnosis in advanced cellular immunodeficiency.**',['The chest radiograph can be less striking than the hypoxemia.','Other infections and noninfectious causes must still be considered.'], '**Obtain organism-directed respiratory testing while treating significant suspected disease.**',['Induced sputum or BAL can provide diagnostic material.','PCR interpretation requires attention to colonization and clinical probability.'],[['Syndrome','Subacute dry cough and hypoxemia'],['Imaging','Often diffuse ground-glass change'],['Confirmation','Respiratory organism detection in context']],[PC],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with HIV-associated Pneumocystis pneumonia has room-air PaO2 62 mmHg. What is appropriate in addition to effective anti-Pneumocystis treatment?', 'Adjunctive corticosteroids started promptly, ideally within 72 hours',[
 ('No corticosteroids unless PaO2 falls below 40 mmHg','The established threshold is less severe than that.'),('Corticosteroids alone without antimicrobial treatment','They do not eradicate Pneumocystis.'),('Routine delay of steroids until the end of antimicrobial treatment','Early use provides the established benefit in qualifying HIV-associated disease.')],
 'Use the hypoxemia criteria for adjunctive treatment', '**For HIV-associated PCP, PaO2 below 70 mmHg or an A–a gradient at least 35 mmHg meets the criterion.**',['The room-air condition matters when applying the threshold.','The evidence should not be automatically generalized to every non-HIV host.'], '**Start appropriate antimicrobial treatment at the same time.**',['TMP-SMX is preferred when usable.','Monitor oxygenation, renal function, electrolytes and drug toxicity.'],[['PaO2','Below 70 mmHg on room air'],['Alternative criterion','A–a gradient at least 35 mmHg'],['Timing','As soon as possible, ideally within 72 hours']],[PC],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which is the preferred initial antimicrobial for most adults with Pneumocystis pneumonia when no contraindication is present?', 'Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole',[
 ('Fluconazole','This does not provide effective Pneumocystis treatment.'),('Ceftriaxone','Standard bacterial CAP coverage does not treat Pneumocystis.'),('Azithromycin alone','It is not an adequate treatment regimen for PCP.')],
 'Pneumocystis requires a specific regimen', '**TMP-SMX is the preferred treatment across disease severities when tolerated.**',['Dose is based on the trimethoprim component and adjusted for renal function.','Route depends on severity and ability to absorb oral treatment.'], '**Manage toxicity without abandoning effective treatment unnecessarily.**',['Monitor potassium, renal function, blood counts and hypersensitivity.','Check G6PD before relevant alternatives such as primaquine or dapsone.'],[['Preferred','TMP-SMX'],['Severity','Guides route and adjunctive steroid assessment'],['Alternatives','Selected by severity and toxicity constraints']],[PC])
q('A patient with prolonged neutropenia has persistent fever and CT nodules with surrounding ground-glass halos. Which infection is a major concern?', 'Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis',[
 ('Simple aspergilloma in a stable old cavity','A fungus ball is a different structural presentation.'),('ABPA as the best explanation for neutropenic nodules','ABPA is a hypersensitivity syndrome, usually in an appropriate airway-disease setting.'),('Uncomplicated acute bronchitis','It does not adequately explain the host risk and nodular CT pattern.')],
 'Host risk changes the significance of a halo sign', '**Angioinvasive mold infection is an urgent consideration in prolonged neutropenia.**',['The halo reflects surrounding hemorrhage or inflammation.','The sign is suggestive rather than organism-specific.'], '**Start appropriate treatment when suspicion is strong while investigating.**',['BAL or tissue and fungal biomarkers can support diagnosis.','Mucorales and nonfungal mimics remain relevant alternatives.'],[['Host','Prolonged neutropenia'],['CT','Nodules with surrounding halo'],['Interpretation','Concern for angioinvasive mold, not proof alone']],[AS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which test is particularly useful as a supportive Aspergillus biomarker in serum or BAL in appropriately selected patients with hematologic malignancy or HSCT?', 'Galactomannan',[
 ('A positive beta-D-glucan as species-specific proof of Aspergillus','Beta-D-glucan is not Aspergillus-specific.'),('Serum ACE as a fungal antigen assay','ACE is not an Aspergillus antigen test.'),('Aspergillus-specific IgE as proof of angioinvasion','IgE indicates sensitization and contributes to allergic-disease assessment.')],
 'Choose a biomarker matched to syndrome and host', '**Galactomannan can support invasive aspergillosis assessment in selected high-risk populations.**',['Diagnostic performance varies with host and specimen.','Mold-active prophylaxis can reduce sensitivity.'], '**Interpret a result with imaging and microbiology.**',['A single negative serum result does not exclude every invasive presentation.','False positives and colonization must be considered.'],[['Galactomannan','Aspergillus-associated antigen'],['Beta-D-glucan','Broader fungal marker'],['Specific IgE','Sensitization / allergic phenotype']],[AS])
q('In proven invasive pulmonary aspergillosis, which drug is an established primary mold-active triazole option?', 'Voriconazole',[
 ('Fluconazole','It lacks reliable Aspergillus activity.'),('Micafungin monotherapy as the usual preferred primary regimen','An echinocandin alone is not the standard primary choice for invasive aspergillosis.'),('Oral nystatin','It is not a systemic treatment for invasive pulmonary disease.')],
 'Select a systemic agent with established Aspergillus activity', '**Voriconazole is an established first-line agent; isavuconazole is an alternative.**',['Consider interactions, organ function and local resistance.','Therapeutic drug monitoring is useful for selected azoles.'], '**Tailor monotherapy or combination strategy to the patient and current guidance.**',['The 2025 ATS guideline addresses mold-active triazole with or without an echinocandin.','This does not make fluconazole or echinocandin-only treatment equivalent primary options.'],[['Voriconazole / isavuconazole','Systemic mold-active triazoles'],['Fluconazole','No reliable Aspergillus coverage'],['Echinocandin alone','Not usual primary therapy']],[AS,AT])
q('A diabetic patient has pulmonary mucormycosis established on tissue. Which statement about voriconazole is correct?', 'It lacks reliable activity against Mucorales and is not an appropriate directed treatment',[
 ('It is interchangeable with liposomal amphotericin B for Mucorales','Their antifungal spectra differ.'),('A positive mold culture guarantees susceptibility to voriconazole','Mold identity and susceptibility matter.'),('A CT halo makes Aspergillus the only possible mold','Imaging cannot reliably distinguish every mold species.')],
 'Mold identification can fundamentally change treatment', '**Voriconazole does not cover Mucorales.**',['Liposomal amphotericin B is a key treatment option for mucormycosis.','Selected other agents and surgery may contribute according to disease extent.'], '**Correct the predisposing state and seek expert-directed treatment promptly.**',['Diabetes and ketoacidosis require active correction.','Obtain microbiological identification rather than treating all molds as interchangeable.'],[['Aspergillus','Voriconazole may be appropriate'],['Mucorales','Different active regimen needed'],['Imaging','Raises suspicion but does not establish species']],[AS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with an old tuberculosis cavity has 5 months of weight loss, cough, enlarging thick-walled cavities and positive Aspergillus IgG. What is the most likely syndrome?', 'Chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis',[
 ('ABPA','Sensitization with an allergic airway phenotype is a different syndrome.'),('Asymptomatic simple aspergilloma only','Progressive cavities and systemic symptoms imply more extensive chronic disease.'),('Acute angioinvasive aspergillosis solely from a positive IgG','IgG with chronic cavitary progression in structural disease fits CPA, not necessarily acute invasion.')],
 'Integrate chronicity, structural disease and Aspergillus evidence', '**CPA commonly complicates damaged lung and evolves over months.**',['Cavitation, pericavitary inflammation and pleural thickening may occur.','Exclude recurrent TB, malignancy and other cavitary diseases.'], '**Treat symptomatic or progressive disease with an appropriate prolonged regimen.**',['Assess azole susceptibility, interactions and drug levels where appropriate.','Hemoptysis and respiratory impairment need separate assessment.'],[['CPA','Chronic progressive cavitary illness'],['Simple aspergilloma','Fungus ball without progressive CPA features'],['ABPA','Allergic airway disease']],[AS,BT],style='clinical_scenario')
q('CT shows a mobile intracavitary mass separated from the wall by air in a pre-existing cavity. The patient has recurrent hemoptysis. Which lesion is most characteristic?', 'Aspergilloma',[
 ('A solid primary tumor established by the air crescent alone','A mobile intracavitary mass favors a fungus ball, although malignancy remains a differential in uncertain cases.'),('Uncomplicated lobar pneumonia','This does not explain the characteristic intracavitary mass.'),('Pleural empyema','That is an infected pleural-space collection rather than an intracavitary fungus ball.')],
 'A fungus ball can colonize a pre-existing cavity', '**An aspergilloma consists of fungal material and debris within a cavity.**',['Movement with position can support the interpretation.','Assess whether there is associated progressive chronic aspergillosis.'], '**Hemoptysis management depends on severity and operability.**',['Major bleeding requires airway and hemodynamic management and often bronchial artery embolization.','Selected localized symptomatic lesions may be treated surgically after risk assessment.'],[['Location','Pre-existing pulmonary cavity'],['Clue','Mobile intracavitary mass'],['Important risk','Hemoptysis']],[AS,BT],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which feature most strongly distinguishes ABPA from uncomplicated Aspergillus airway sensitization?', 'A compatible multidomain syndrome with immunologic activity and supportive imaging rather than specific IgE alone',[
 ('Positive Aspergillus-specific IgE by itself','Sensitization alone does not establish ABPA.'),('Isolation of Aspergillus from one sputum sample alone','Colonization is possible and does not define an allergic syndrome.'),('Positive serum galactomannan as a mandatory ABPA criterion','Galactomannan is not the defining ABPA diagnostic framework.')],
 'Allergy, colonization and invasion are different syndromes', '**ABPA requires more than evidence of sensitization.**',['Use a current diagnostic framework that integrates total IgE and other supportive features.','Bronchiectasis or mucus plugging can support the airway phenotype.'], '**Choose treatment according to the actual syndrome.**',['An allergic inflammatory process and invasive infection need different strategies.','A positive test must be interpreted in its clinical setting.'],[['Specific IgE alone','Sensitization'],['ABPA','Compatible allergic multisystem evidence'],['Invasive disease','Host, imaging and invasive microbiological evidence']],[AS,BT])
q('A sputum culture from a critically ill patient grows Candida, but there is no evidence of invasive candidiasis. What is the most appropriate interpretation?', 'Airway colonization is much more likely than Candida pneumonia, so the culture alone is not an indication for systemic antifungals',[
 ('Any Candida respiratory isolate proves invasive pneumonia','Respiratory colonization is common.'),('The result establishes candidemia','A respiratory specimen is not a blood culture.'),('Fluconazole is mandatory regardless of the clinical context','Treatment should target established or sufficiently suspected invasive disease, not this result alone.')],
 'Respiratory yeast isolation is not synonymous with tissue invasion', '**Candida in airway specimens usually represents colonization.**',['Consider host risk and evidence of disseminated infection.','A rare invasive pulmonary diagnosis requires stronger evidence.'], '**Investigate other causes of deterioration.**',['Bacterial pneumonia, edema and other fungal pathogens may be more relevant.','Avoid unnecessary antifungal toxicity and resistance pressure.'],[['Respiratory culture','Often colonization'],['Invasive diagnosis','Requires additional evidence'],['Therapy','Not triggered by airway isolate alone']],[AT],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A transplant recipient has cavitary pulmonary nodules and a brain abscess. Respiratory microscopy shows branching, weakly acid-fast organisms. Which pathogen is most likely?', 'Nocardia',[
 ('Actinomyces','It is branching but generally not weakly acid-fast; the dissemination pattern in this host favors Nocardia.'),('Aspergillus','It forms fungal hyphae rather than weakly acid-fast branching bacteria.'),('Legionella pneumophila','It does not produce this characteristic branching microscopy and brain-abscess pattern.')],
 'Connect pulmonary nocardiosis with CNS dissemination', '**Nocardia is an opportunistic branching bacterial pathogen.**',['Pulmonary disease may cavitate.','The brain is an important dissemination site.'], '**Alert the laboratory and use species and susceptibility information.**',['Extended incubation and appropriate identification may be needed.','Severe or CNS disease often requires an initially combined, prolonged regimen selected with specialist input.'],[['Morphology','Branching, weakly acid-fast bacteria'],['Host','Cellular immunosuppression is important'],['Dissemination','CNS abscess is a key concern']],[NC],style='clinical_scenario')

